IAM Permissions
Each user can be given to the least permissions they needed using the policies.
Example if a user just want to describe instances, list s3 buckets and view cloudwatch logs we can just assign that permissions
Assigning each user to a group is a best practice
These policies are in JSON format
These policies define the permissions of the users [missed]


IAM Policies Inheritance
A policy can be assigned directly to a user using inline policies
A policy can be assigned to a group and the group can be assigned to a user
Multiple policy can be assigned to multiple group based on requirements and multiple groups can be assigned to a single user

IAM Policy structure
Version - used to mention the version of the policy
Id - an identifier for the policy (optional)
Statement - Where sid, effect, action, principal and resource takes place
	sid - an identifier for statement (optional)
	effect - used to mention is the actions are allowed or denied
	action - the actions such as describe ec2 instances
	principal - specifies the aws identities (user, account or role) this policy appied for | mostly principal is not used for identity based policy its mostly used for resource based policy
	resource - which resource this statement applied for

IAM Password Policy / MFA
A user need to set stronger password
We can enforce a user to set password with
	certain character length
	consists of non alphanumeric characters
	consist of lower class or higher class letters
	consists of numbers etc
We can allow the user to reset password
Enforce the user to change password after certain period
MFA
MFA is used as second step verification for example a user have a password, a security device (such as authenticator on mobile).
If a user password is compromised the hacker still need to have access of the security device to generate MFA token
We can enforce the user to use MFA


