Day 3: 10-9

IAM Advanced

AWS Organization
AWS Organization is used to manage aws accounts centralized
It has two accounts Management and Member account
	Management account is used to manage every thing under an AWS Organization
	Member account is inside the AWS Organization managed by organization which can be under an OU itself and each member can only be a part of on Organization
Every accounts and OU is under Root OU
The billing from every accounts can be consiladated in one single bill

SCP [Service Control Policy]
Service control policy is used to grant maximum level of permission to a OU it doesn't grant permission but guardrail what are the actions they can do
To do a action they should also have the IAM Permission of that action
Example:
Production OU [SCP: Deny EC2]
	Account A [IAM: Allow EC2]
	Account B
if the Account A tries to create an EC2 instance it cant be created as SCP Denied it, to create EC2 instance the account should have Allow EC2 and SCP should not deny that
SCP policies not applicable for Management account

Easy way to remember
AWS Organizations = manages multiple AWS accounts
OU = groups AWS accounts
SCP = organizational ceiling/guardrail
IAM = actually grants permissions

AWS Organizations - Tag policies
Helps to standardize tagged policies
Tag policies is to standardize the name that applies on organization level that has key - value pair 
owner - payment team 
environment - production etc 
by using the tag policies we can also check is every resources are compliant later it can also helps the organization on billing

Advanced IAM Policies
AWS Resource policies
A resource-based policy is a policy attached directly to a resource that controls who can access that resource and what they can do.
Example: resources like S3, EC2 etc to deny access to certain Regions, IP or MFA based access to Member accounts
aws:PrincipalOrgID
Its can be applied for any resource based policies to restrict access based on accounts that are member of an AWS organization

